// HARD Question Bank - Domain 4.0 (part B: 4.5-4.9)
window.HARD_BANK = (window.HARD_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 4.5 (22) ----------
{o:"4.5",q:"A company's SPF record ends in '~all' and its DMARC policy is 'p=none'. Spoofed mail using the company domain keeps reaching partners' inboxes, and leadership wants spoofing stopped without blocking legitimate mail. What is the BEST remediation sequence?",c:["Set DMARC to p=reject immediately, then fix any legitimate senders that bounce based on helpdesk tickets","Review DMARC reports, fix SPF/DKIM alignment for each legitimate sender, then step DMARC up to reject","Change SPF to '-all', publish DKIM keys for the domain, and leave DMARC at p=none to avoid any false positives","Add the partners' mail servers to the SPF record, enable DMARC forensic reports, then move DMARC to quarantine"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Moving to reject before the sender inventory is complete bounces legitimate mail from any unaligned system, which the stem says must not happen. Immediate reject is acceptable only after reports confirm every legitimate sender passes.","Correct. DMARC reports show every system sending as the domain, and aligning SPF/DKIM for each first means enforcement will not block legitimate mail. Stepping to quarantine and then reject ends the spoofing the partners are seeing.","Incorrect. A stricter SPF and DKIM publishing help, but p=none tells receivers to take no action on failures, so spoofed mail is still delivered. Staying at p=none is appropriate only during the initial monitoring phase.","Incorrect. SPF lists the servers allowed to send for your domain, so adding partners' servers authorizes strangers and does nothing about spoofers. Forensic reports help, but the sender inventory and alignment fixes are skipped."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Marketing sends newsletters as @company.com through a third-party platform. Within an hour of the company moving DMARC to p=reject, every newsletter bounces while employee mail is unaffected. What is the MOST likely cause?",c:["The newsletters exceed the receiving servers' message size limit because the platform embeds images inline","The company's MX records do not list the platform, so receivers reject mail from servers that are not named mail exchangers","The platform's sending IP has no PTR record matching its HELO name, which DMARC evaluates before checking alignment","The platform's IPs are missing from SPF and it signs DKIM with its own domain, so neither aligns with company.com"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Size-limit bounces occur at the receiving server independent of DMARC and would have been happening before the policy change. A size cause would be suspected if only the largest mailings failed.","Incorrect. MX records tell senders where to deliver inbound mail for your domain; receivers do not check outbound senders against them. MX errors would be suspected if mail addressed to the company failed to arrive.","Incorrect. Reverse-DNS mismatches affect spam scoring at some receivers but are not part of DMARC evaluation, and they would not coincide exactly with the policy change. DMARC looks only at SPF and DKIM alignment.","Correct. Employee mail aligns, but the vendor's mail passes neither aligned SPF nor aligned DKIM, so the new reject policy applies only to it. The fix is to add the vendor to SPF and delegate or publish a DKIM key for company.com."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"An analyst reviews a received message: SPF=pass for mailer.evil.com, DKIM=pass with d=evil.com, visible From: ceo@company.com, and DMARC=fail. Why did DMARC fail?",c:["Neither the SPF-authenticated domain nor the DKIM d= domain aligns with the From domain, company.com","DMARC requires SPF and DKIM to both align, and only one of the two mechanisms aligned with company.com","The receiver applied evil.com's published DMARC policy, which was stricter than the policy published for company.com","The Return-Path domain differed from the HELO domain, and DMARC fails any message whose envelope identities disagree"],a:0,e:["Correct. DMARC passes only when SPF or DKIM passes and the authenticated domain aligns with the visible From domain. Both passes here are for evil.com, so neither aligns with company.com and DMARC fails.","Incorrect. DMARC needs only one aligned pass, not both, and here neither aligned. A both-must-align rule would be stricter than any real DMARC implementation.","Incorrect. Receivers evaluate the policy published by the From header domain, which is company.com. evil.com's own policy would matter only for mail whose visible From address was at evil.com.","Incorrect. DMARC compares the authenticated domains with the From header; it does not require envelope identities to agree with each other. Such mismatches may affect spam scoring but do not define a DMARC failure."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Phishing campaigns increasingly use domains registered hours earlier, so they appear on no blocklist or threat feed. Which control BEST blocks the first victims' clicks?",c:["A static URL deny list refreshed daily from user-reported phishing feeds and vendor threat-intelligence sources","TLS inspection that blocks any site presenting a self-signed, expired or mismatched-name certificate","Web/DNS filtering that blocks newly registered domains by age, with URL detonation for first-seen links","Egress firewall rules that drop traffic to IP ranges of hosting providers with poor abuse histories"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Deny lists depend on someone reporting the site first, so brand-new domains slip through until the next refresh. They suit persistent known-bad infrastructure rather than hours-old domains.","Incorrect. Phishing sites routinely use free, valid certificates, so certificate state is not a useful discriminator. This control suits spotting misconfigured services or intercepted connections.","Correct. A domain's age is known the moment it is registered, and detonating first-seen links evaluates unknown pages, so neither depends on a blocklist. That is what the stem's no-blocklist clue demands.","Incorrect. Attackers host on mainstream clouds and CDNs, and blocking whole ranges breaks legitimate sites. Range blocking fits persistent abuse originating from a known bulletproof hosting provider."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A company enforces DNS filtering on its resolvers, yet malware resolves C2 names using DNS over HTTPS to a public provider on TCP 443. Which countermeasure BEST closes this gap?",c:["Enable DNSSEC validation on corporate resolvers and block outbound TCP/UDP 53 to every non-corporate destination","Sinkhole known-malicious domains on the corporate resolvers and block outbound UDP/TCP 53 to external servers","Block known public DoH endpoints, force clients to corporate resolvers, and inspect egress TLS for DoH on 443","Deploy a response policy zone fed by threat intelligence and enforce DNS over TLS on port 853 internally"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. DNSSEC authenticates answers but does not stop clients from using another resolver, and closing port 53 never touches DoH on 443. This pairing suits stopping classic plaintext resolver bypass.","Incorrect. The malware never asks the corporate resolver, so the sinkhole is bypassed, and port 53 rules do not affect 443. It works against clients that use standard DNS queries.","Correct. The bypass travels over TCP 443, so the fix must control which resolvers are reachable and detect DoH inside HTTPS. Forcing clients to corporate resolvers restores filtering.","Incorrect. A response policy zone only affects queries that reach your resolver, and DNS over TLS on 853 does not stop DoH. It helps harden internal resolver traffic against eavesdropping."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"File integrity monitoring reports that the hosts file changed on 50 workstations overnight. No change ticket or software deployment matches, and only the endpoints' local file contents changed. What does this MOST likely indicate?",c:["Local DNS redirection (pharming) by malware or an unauthorized script editing the hosts file","An approved configuration-management push of internal host entries from the endpoint team","DNS cache poisoning of the corporate upstream resolver by an external attacker","ARP spoofing by a rogue device on the workstation VLAN redirecting local traffic"],a:0,e:["Correct. The hosts file overrides DNS locally, so an unplanned coordinated edit across fifty endpoints with no ticket points to tampering used to redirect traffic.","Incorrect. This would be the right reading if a change ticket or deployment job matched the edit. With no record of an approved change, a coordinated edit must be treated as hostile until proven otherwise.","Incorrect. Poisoning corrupts a resolver's cache and does not modify files on endpoints. It would be suspected if name resolution was wrong while the local files were unchanged.","Incorrect. ARP spoofing redirects traffic on the wire without writing to any file on the hosts, and FIM would not alert on it. Dynamic ARP inspection or network IDS would catch it."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Which DLP deployment would detect confidential files being copied to a USB drive while an employee's laptop is disconnected from every network?",c:["Network DLP sensor on the egress gateway","API-based CASB DLP for sanctioned SaaS","Email gateway DLP with outbound attachment scanning","Endpoint DLP agent with device-control policy"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A gateway sees only traffic crossing it, and an offline laptop generates none. Network DLP fits monitoring data leaving through web, FTP or similar sessions.","Incorrect. CASB DLP inspects data inside cloud applications and cannot see local USB writes. It fits preventing sensitive files from being shared out of services like Box or Microsoft 365.","Incorrect. Email DLP examines messages as they pass through the mail system. It suits catching sensitive data sent by email, not data copied to removable storage.","Correct. Only software on the device sees file operations and removable-media use while it is offline, and it can queue alerts or block the copy by policy until the laptop reconnects."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Firewall rule request: 'Allow the Nurse Workstation (192.168.100.10) to retrieve messages from the Pharmacy System (172.25.10.20) using IMAP, unencrypted.' Which entry implements it?",c:["Src 172.25.10.20 → Dst 192.168.100.10, TCP 143, Allow","Src 192.168.100.10 → Dst 172.25.10.20, TCP 143, Allow","Src 192.168.100.10 → Dst 172.25.10.20, TCP 993, Allow","Src 192.168.100.10 → Dst 172.25.10.20, TCP 110, Allow"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the direction, letting the pharmacy system open sessions to the workstation. It would fit only if the server pushed data to a listener on the client.","Correct. The workstation initiates toward the pharmacy server, and unencrypted IMAP listens on TCP 143, so source is the workstation and destination port is 143.","Incorrect. TCP 993 is IMAP over implicit TLS, but the request specifies unencrypted IMAP. It would be correct if the requirement said secure IMAP.","Incorrect. TCP 110 is POP3, a different mail retrieval protocol. It would be correct if the request named POP3."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Requirement: 'Block all Internet access from the entire ICS zone (10.50.100.0/24).' Which entry satisfies it?",c:["Src Internet → Dst 10.50.100.0/24, Any port, Any protocol, Deny","Src 10.50.100.0/24 → Dst Internet, Any port/protocol, Deny","Src 10.50.100.0/24 → Dst Internet, TCP 80 and 443, Deny","Src 10.50.100.20 → Dst Internet, Any port, Any protocol, Deny"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. This blocks inbound traffic only, so ICS hosts could still initiate outbound sessions to the Internet. It would fit a requirement to shield the zone from external scans.","Correct. Traffic originates inside the ICS subnet heading outbound, and the requirement covers every port and protocol, so the entry must use the subnet as source with any port.","Incorrect. The direction and scope are right, but only web ports are covered, leaving DNS, NTP, SSH and others open. It fits a requirement to block only web browsing.","Incorrect. A single host does not cover the entire /24 zone. It fits a requirement to restrict one specific device, such as an engineering workstation."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Requirement: 'Allow the Application Server (192.168.50.30) to connect to the Oracle Database (192.168.50.20) listener on its default port, nothing else.' Which port and protocol belong in the rule?",c:["TCP 1433 (SQL Server default instance)","TCP 3306 (MySQL default)","TCP 5432 (PostgreSQL default)","TCP 1521 (Oracle Net listener)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. TCP 1433 is the Microsoft SQL Server default instance port. It would be correct if the back end were SQL Server.","Incorrect. TCP 3306 is the MySQL and MariaDB default. It would be correct for a MySQL database server.","Incorrect. TCP 5432 is the PostgreSQL default. It would be correct if the application used a PostgreSQL database.","Correct. The Oracle Net listener defaults to TCP 1521, and the rule should permit only that port from the application server to the database."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A firewall's ordered rules: 1) Allow Any → Web server TCP 443; 2) Deny 203.0.113.9 → Any; 3) Deny Any → Any. The attacker at 203.0.113.9 reaches the web server on 443. What happens and what is the fix?",c:["Rule 1 allows it because first match wins; move the specific deny above the broad allow","Rule 2 blocks it because the most specific rule always wins regardless of order; no change is needed","Rule 1 allows it because allow rules outrank deny rules; convert rule 3 into an explicit permit list","Rule 3 blocks it because the final deny catches unmatched sources; move rule 3 to the top"],a:0,e:["Correct. Rules are evaluated top-down and the first match wins, so rule 1 allows the attacker before rule 2 is reached. The specific deny must move above the broad allow.","Incorrect. Most firewalls evaluate in order, not by specificity, so rule 2 is never reached for this traffic. Specificity-based matching exists only in some routing and policy engines.","Incorrect. Allow rules do not inherently outrank deny rules; order decides. Rule 3 is already the proper implicit-deny backstop, and converting it into a permit would weaken the policy.","Incorrect. Rule 1 matches first, so the final deny never evaluates for this traffic. Moving a deny-all to the top would block every legitimate user as well."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"An Apache process on Linux must be unable to read /home even though those files are world-readable, and the control must prevent access rather than log it. Which measure BEST achieves this?",c:["Group-based POSIX permissions that remove the apache user from groups owning home directories","Auditd watch rules on /home that record reads by the apache user","An SELinux or AppArmor profile that denies the Apache domain access to home-directory paths","Dropping Linux capabilities such as CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE from the Apache service unit"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. World-readable files grant access to 'other', so removing group membership changes nothing. Group-based permissions work for files that are not world-readable.","Incorrect. Auditd is detective: it records access but does not block it. It fits requirements to detect or investigate access rather than prevent it.","Correct. Mandatory access control policy confines the process regardless of file permissions, so world-readable files stay unreadable to Apache. It also actually prevents access, as the stem requires.","Incorrect. Capabilities such as CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE bypass permission checks, but reading world-readable files needs none, so dropping them blocks nothing. It fits hardening against reading protected files."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A Group Policy blocks execution of binaries from %AppData% and %Temp%. Which threat does this MOST directly reduce?",c:["Pass-the-hash lateral movement using stolen NTLM hashes","Kernel rootkits loaded through signed vulnerable drivers","Commodity droppers launched from user-writable folders","Credential stuffing against cloud-hosted portals"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Pass-the-hash replays credentials over the network and does not depend on executing files from user folders. Credential Guard and NTLM restrictions address it.","Incorrect. Driver abuse loads code from system locations with administrator rights. It is mitigated by driver blocklists and HVCI rather than user-folder execution rules.","Correct. These paths are writable without administrator rights and are common landing spots, so path-based execution control interrupts a large share of commodity malware.","Incorrect. Credential stuffing happens at an application login and involves no local execution. MFA and rate limiting address it."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"An inline IPS in signature mode misses an obfuscated attack, so the team enables anomaly detection and sees heavy false positives in week one. Leadership insists on retaining inline prevention. What is the BEST next step?",c:["Baseline normal traffic during a learning period, then tune thresholds and exclusions before enforcing","Revert the sensor to signature-only mode and rely on vendor updates for obfuscated attacks","Move the sensor out of band in IDS mode permanently and review its alerts on a monthly basis","Increase anomaly sensitivity so the engine learns from more detections and fewer true events slip through"],a:0,e:["Correct. Anomaly engines need a baseline and tuning; doing this keeps the coverage that catches obfuscated attacks while cutting noise, and it preserves the inline prevention leadership requires.","Incorrect. Reverting to signature-only restores the exact blind spot that missed the obfuscated attack. Signature-only is acceptable where tuning resources do not exist and risk is low.","Incorrect. A permanent out-of-band deployment abandons inline prevention that leadership requires. Temporary IDS mode during tuning is fine, but monthly review delays response.","Incorrect. Higher sensitivity raises the false-positive rate that caused the problem in the first place. Increasing sensitivity suits environments where missed detections cost more than noise."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A company wants to stop users reaching uncategorized sites while keeping business-critical SaaS available, even when SaaS vendors change hosting. Which approach is BEST?",c:["Application-layer firewall rules allowing only the SaaS vendors' published IP address ranges","Reputation filtering that blocks any site scoring below a threshold, with no exception list","DNS sinkholing of every domain outside a top-million popularity ranking","Category filtering that blocks Uncategorized sites, plus an allow list of approved SaaS domains"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Vendor IP ranges shift and overlap with CDNs, causing outages or gaps whenever hosting changes. IP allow lists suit stable, dedicated address space.","Incorrect. Without exceptions, legitimate SaaS with poor or new reputation scores breaks, and uncategorized sites may score neutral. Reputation scoring complements categories rather than replacing allow lists.","Incorrect. A popularity cutoff blocks many legitimate niche SaaS vendors and says nothing about category. It is a blunt heuristic more suited to hunting rarely seen domains.","Correct. Domain-based categories and an explicit allow list follow SaaS vendors across hosting changes while blocking unknown destinations, which meets both goals."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Which email gateway capability BEST detects a weaponized .docx attachment that no antivirus signature or reputation feed has seen before?",c:["Hash reputation lookup against threat-intelligence feeds","Attachment sandboxing with behavioral detonation of the file","DKIM verification of the sending domain's signature","File-extension and MIME-type blocking for executables and scripts"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A never-before-seen file has no reputation, so the lookup returns nothing. Hash reputation is effective for known samples.","Correct. Dynamic analysis reveals malicious behavior, such as a macro launching a process, without needing prior knowledge of the file, which is exactly what an unseen .docx requires.","Incorrect. DKIM proves the message was signed by the domain, not that the attachment is safe. A validly signed message can carry malware.","Incorrect. A .docx is a permitted business file type, so extension rules pass it. Blocklists work for file types the business never needs."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A rule must allow a File Server (192.168.50.10) to forward logs over encrypted syslog to an Internet logging service. Which entry is correct?",c:["Src 192.168.50.10 → Dst Internet, UDP 514, Allow","Src 192.168.50.10 → Dst Internet, TCP 6514, Allow","Src Internet → Dst 192.168.50.10, TCP 6514, Allow (inbound)","Src 192.168.50.10 → Dst Internet, TCP 514, Allow"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. UDP 514 is traditional plaintext syslog, which fails the encrypted requirement. It would fit an internal collector where encryption is not needed.","Correct. Syslog over TLS uses TCP 6514, and the file server initiates the connection outbound to the logging service.","Incorrect. This reverses the direction and would expose the file server to inbound connections. It would apply if the file server were the collector.","Incorrect. TCP 514 carries syslog without TLS, so logs would cross the Internet in cleartext. It is acceptable only on a trusted internal path."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A rule must let the Backup Server reach the File Server using modern SMB with direct hosting, without NetBIOS. Which port and protocol apply?",c:["TCP 139 (NetBIOS session)","UDP 138 (NetBIOS datagram)","TCP 135 (RPC endpoint mapper)","TCP 445 (SMB direct hosting)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. TCP 139 carries SMB over the NetBIOS session service, which the requirement excludes. It remains for legacy clients.","Incorrect. UDP 138 is the NetBIOS datagram service used for browsing and broadcasts, not file access. It matters for legacy network browsing.","Incorrect. TCP 135 is the RPC endpoint mapper, used by DCOM and some Windows management tasks rather than file transfer. It is needed for certain remote administration features.","Correct. SMB with direct hosting runs over TCP 445 and needs no NetBIOS layer, which matches the requirement."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Users can reach both the corporate and personal OneDrive tenants on the same domain. Which technology can allow uploads to the corporate tenant but block them to personal tenants?",c:["CASB with tenant-aware (instance-aware) policy","Next-generation firewall application identification for OneDrive","Secure web gateway URL category block of file-sharing sites","Endpoint DLP keyword rules on documents and uploads"],a:0,e:["Correct. Tenant restrictions let the control distinguish corporate from personal instances of the same application on the same domain, and control specific actions such as uploads.","Incorrect. App-ID can allow or block OneDrive as an application but treats both tenants alike. It fits allowing a SaaS category wholesale.","Incorrect. A category block would also stop the corporate tenant since both share the domain. It suits requirements to ban a service entirely.","Incorrect. Keyword rules look at content, not which tenant receives it. They suit preventing sensitive data from leaving through any channel."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Which scenario is a UEBA platform MOST likely to flag that a signature-based system would miss?",c:["A workstation executing a binary whose hash matches a known ransomware family","An external host sending SYN packets to sequential ports across the DMZ","An accountant's valid account querying the HR database at 3 a.m.","A public web server presenting a TLS certificate that expired last night"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A known hash is the classic signature detection that endpoint protection already catches. Behavioral analytics add little here.","Incorrect. Port scans are detected by IDS/IPS and firewall rate rules. UEBA focuses on authenticated user or entity behavior.","Correct. Nothing malicious appears in the traffic itself; the anomaly is a deviation from this user's and peer group's baseline, which is what UEBA models.","Incorrect. Expiry is a deterministic condition caught by certificate monitoring. It involves no behavioral deviation by a user or entity."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"An RDP service is exposed to the Internet for remote administration. Which change MOST reduces risk without breaking admin workflows?",c:["Move RDP to a non-standard TCP port and enable Network Level Authentication on the exposed host","Keep RDP public but geo-restrict the rule to the home country and add an account lockout policy","Remove public exposure, require VPN or ZTNA with MFA, and limit RDP to the admin subnet","Keep RDP public but allow-list administrators' home ISP ranges and require NLA"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Scanners find alternate ports within hours, and the service stays reachable by anyone. NLA helps but does not remove exposure.","Incorrect. Attackers use in-country proxies, and lockout policies enable denial of service against admins. Geo-restriction reduces noise but not targeted risk.","Correct. Removing direct exposure and brokering access through VPN or ZTNA with MFA eliminates the attack surface while preserving admin access.","Incorrect. Home ISP ranges are large and shared, so many unrelated hosts still reach the service. Tight, fixed source addresses would be more defensible."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Which rule correctly allows IPsec VPN tunnels from the Internet to a VPN gateway at 10.10.10.40, including NAT traversal?",c:["Src Internet → Dst 10.10.10.40, UDP 500/4500, Allow","Src Internet → Dst 10.10.10.40, TCP 443, Allow","Src Internet → Dst 10.10.10.40, TCP 500, Allow","Src 10.10.10.40 → Dst Internet, UDP 500 and 4500, Allow"],a:0,e:["Correct. IKE negotiates on UDP 500 and NAT traversal encapsulation uses UDP 4500, both inbound to the gateway from the Internet.","Incorrect. TCP 443 is for SSL/TLS VPNs, not IPsec IKE. It would be correct for a clientless or SSL VPN portal.","Incorrect. IKE uses UDP, not TCP, so this rule would never match the negotiation. The port number is right but the protocol is wrong.","Incorrect. This allows the gateway to initiate outbound, but remote clients connect inbound. It fits a site-to-site tunnel where the gateway initiates."]},

// ---------- 4.6 (22) ----------
{o:"4.6",q:"A company federates with a partner using SAML: partner employees authenticate at their own organization and then open an application you host. What role does YOUR application play?",c:["Identity provider (asserting party) that authenticates users and issues assertions","Policy decision point that evaluates access requests against central rules","Certificate authority that vouches for the partner's signing key","Service provider (relying party) that consumes the partner IdP's assertions"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The identity provider authenticates users and issues assertions, and here that is the partner. Your app would be the IdP only if you authenticated the users yourself.","Incorrect. A policy decision point is a component of an authorization architecture, not the SAML role of a consuming application. It fits ABAC and zero trust designs.","Incorrect. A certificate authority issues certificates and has no role in the SAML flow beyond optionally anchoring trust in signing keys. It would apply to the PKI behind the assertions.","Correct. The partner authenticates its own users and issues assertions, and your application consumes them, which is the service provider (relying party) role."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A mobile app must call a user's Google Drive API on the user's behalf and also learn who the user is. Which combination is correct?",c:["OAuth 2.0 for both API authorization and authentication, using the access token as proof of identity","OAuth 2.0 access tokens for the Drive API plus OpenID Connect ID tokens for user identity","SAML assertions for the Drive API plus OAuth 2.0 for user identity","OpenID Connect for Drive API authorization plus Kerberos tickets for user identity"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. OAuth alone is an authorization framework; access tokens are not designed to identify the user. OIDC supplies the standardized identity layer on top.","Correct. OAuth 2.0 delegates access to the Drive API through access tokens, and OpenID Connect adds an ID token that tells the app who the user is.","Incorrect. This swaps the roles: SAML is an authentication standard that is awkward for mobile API delegation, and OAuth does not authenticate users by itself.","Incorrect. OIDC authenticates users but does not delegate API scopes, and Kerberos is for domain environments rather than mobile apps calling a web API."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A security team wants the authenticator MOST resistant to real-time phishing proxies. Which should they deploy?",c:["Push notification MFA with number matching and location context","FIDO2/WebAuthn keys or passkeys bound to the origin","Time-based one-time passwords from an authenticator app","SMS one-time passcodes sent to registered phones"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Number matching defeats prompt bombing, but a proxy can still relay the session the user approves. It fits environments that cannot issue hardware keys.","Correct. A FIDO2/WebAuthn credential only responds to the genuine site origin, so an attacker-in-the-middle proxy cannot relay it. That makes it the most phishing-resistant choice.","Incorrect. TOTP codes can be typed into a phishing page and replayed within their validity window. They are an upgrade over SMS but are not phishing-resistant.","Incorrect. SMS codes can be relayed by proxies and intercepted through SIM swapping. They are acceptable only as a low-assurance fallback."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Which pairing represents TRUE multifactor authentication?",c:["Password plus security question","Fingerprint scan plus iris scan","Proximity badge plus OTP token","Smart card plus a memorized PIN"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Both a password and a security question are knowledge factors, so this is single-factor with two secrets. It would count as multifactor only with a possession or inherence factor.","Incorrect. Both fingerprint and iris scans are inherence (biometric) factors. Two biometrics improve accuracy but remain one factor category.","Incorrect. A badge and an OTP token are both possession factors, so an attacker who steals the bag gets both. Adding a PIN or biometric would make it multifactor.","Correct. A smart card is something you have and the PIN is something you know, which are two different factor categories."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A policy grants access when user.department=Finance AND device.compliant=true AND the time is 07:00-19:00 AND data.classification is at most Confidential. Which access control model is this?",c:["Attribute-based access control (ABAC) policy","Role-based access control (RBAC) by job function","Rule-based access control (time and location rules)","Mandatory access control (MAC) with clearance labels"],a:0,e:["Correct. The decision evaluates attributes of the user, device, environment and resource together, which defines attribute-based access control.","Incorrect. RBAC grants access by role membership and would not evaluate device compliance, time or data classification. It fits stable job functions.","Incorrect. Rule-based control applies fixed rules such as time or location restrictions, not combinations of user, device and data attributes. It would fit a simple schedule restriction.","Incorrect. MAC compares clearance labels to classification labels set by the system, not department or device state. It fits military-style multilevel systems."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"In a system where a Secret-cleared user tries to open a Top Secret document, even the document's creator cannot grant access. Why?",c:["The creator's ACL excludes Secret-cleared users, and ACLs are set by the owner","The user's role lacks the Top Secret permission, and roles map to permissions","Labels and clearances are enforced centrally, so owners cannot override them","The attribute policy evaluates department and denies by default"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Owner-set ACLs describe DAC, where the creator could simply edit the list. The stem stresses that the creator cannot grant access.","Incorrect. Role-based decisions do not depend on clearance labels, and an administrator could assign a role the permission, which MAC forbids.","Correct. Labels and clearances are enforced centrally, so even the creator cannot override them. That owner-proof enforcement is the defining feature of mandatory access control.","Incorrect. ABAC decisions use flexible attributes the policy author chooses. It is not described by fixed owner-proof clearance labels."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A newly promoted manager keeps old team access and gains new access. An audit six months later finds excess permissions across many employees who changed roles. Which process BEST prevents this?",c:["Mandatory 60-day password rotation plus lockout after failed attempts","Just-in-time elevation for managers with full session recording and approval","Access recertification plus mover workflows that remove prior-role access","Step-up MFA on all sensitive applications, enforced for every user and device"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Credential hygiene does not remove entitlements, so excess access would remain. Rotation and lockout fit reducing password guessing and reuse.","Incorrect. Just-in-time elevation limits standing privilege for admin tasks but does not clean up ordinary entitlements from previous roles. It fits privileged account control.","Correct. Recertification catches accumulated access, and mover workflows remove prior-role entitlements at the moment someone changes jobs, stopping privilege creep at its source.","Incorrect. MFA strengthens authentication but leaves excess entitlements in place for anyone who authenticates. It fits protecting high-risk apps from credential theft."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Administrators must receive elevated rights for one hour after approval, and the underlying credentials must rotate afterward. Which capability set provides this?",c:["PAM with just-in-time elevation and vaulted credentials rotated after checkout","SSO with a federated identity provider and conditional access policies","Self-service password reset with identity verification and account unlock","RBAC with quarterly role reviews and permanent group membership"],a:0,e:["Correct. Time-boxed elevation with approval and automatic credential rotation after checkout are core privileged access management features.","Incorrect. SSO centralizes login for ordinary users and does not time-box or rotate privileged credentials. It fits reducing password sprawl.","Incorrect. SSPR lets users recover their own accounts. It does not grant temporary privilege or rotate shared admin secrets.","Incorrect. Permanent group membership is the standing privilege this requirement aims to remove. Role reviews fit governance of ordinary entitlements."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Short-lived (ephemeral) credentials are issued to reduce theft risk, but a team sets their lifetime to 30 days. What is the PRIMARY risk of this configuration?",c:["Frequent re-issuance overloads the identity provider and causes authentication outages","The credentials can no longer be revoked, because revocation only works on credentials that expire quickly","The credentials bind permanently to the first device, so users cannot sign in from a second device","A stolen credential stays usable for a long window, undermining the benefit of short lifetimes"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Overload is a risk of very short lifetimes, the opposite of this configuration. It is why lifetimes are balanced against scale.","Incorrect. Revocation capability does not depend on lifetime; short expiry complements revocation but is not required for it.","Incorrect. Device binding is a separate design choice unrelated to lifetime. It fits defending against token replay on other machines.","Correct. A stolen credential's value to an attacker is proportional to its remaining lifetime, so a 30-day window erases the main advantage of ephemeral credentials."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"An attacker phishes a user's SSO password but MFA stops the login. Later, malware on the user's laptop steals the browser session cookie. What is the risk?",c:["MFA fatigue: the malware triggers repeated push prompts until the user approves one","Session token replay: the stolen cookie is already authenticated, so MFA is bypassed until expiry","Credential stuffing: the cookie contains the password hash, which the attacker cracks offline","Kerberoasting: the cookie lets the attacker request service tickets and crack service account passwords"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. MFA fatigue spams push prompts during authentication. Here the attacker already holds a valid session and needs no prompt.","Correct. MFA protects the login event, but a stolen post-authentication cookie is accepted as proof of the completed login until it expires or is revoked.","Incorrect. Session cookies do not contain password hashes, and stuffing replays leaked username-password pairs against logins. It would fit reused credentials from a breach.","Incorrect. Kerberoasting abuses domain service tickets from Active Directory and has no relation to web session cookies."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Which password policy BEST aligns with current NIST guidance?",c:["12 characters with complexity rules, 90-day forced rotation, and a 24-password history","15+ character passphrases, breached-password screening, MFA, and rotation only after compromise","8 characters with complexity, 30-day rotation, and security questions for account recovery","16 characters with password hints stored for helpdesk use, rotated every 180 days, plus MFA"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Forced periodic rotation and composition rules were dropped because they encourage predictable changes. This reflects older enterprise baselines.","Correct. Length, screening against known-compromised values and MFA replace composition rules and scheduled rotation, which current NIST guidance discourages absent evidence of compromise.","Incorrect. Security questions are weak knowledge-based answers, and short, frequently rotated passwords push users toward patterns. This is the oldest style of policy.","Incorrect. Hints leak the secret and periodic rotation without cause is no longer recommended. The length and MFA are good, but the other elements undermine them."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Domain applications query a directory over LDAP on port 389 using simple binds. What is the risk and the fix?",c:["Anonymous binds expose the directory tree; disable anonymous access and require signing","Bind credentials are cached in plaintext on clients; clear the credential manager vault","LDAP referrals leak internal hostnames; disable referral chasing on the clients","Credentials cross the network in cleartext; use LDAPS on 636 or StartTLS on 389"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Anonymous access is a separate misconfiguration; the scenario uses authenticated simple binds. It fits a directory that returns data without credentials.","Incorrect. Client caching is unrelated to what crosses the wire on port 389. It fits endpoint credential-theft concerns.","Incorrect. Referral behavior discloses topology, not passwords. It is a minor information leak compared to cleartext credentials.","Correct. A simple bind transmits the password unencrypted unless the channel is protected, so use LDAPS on 636 or StartTLS on 389."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Which process gap MOST directly creates orphaned accounts?",c:["Manual, ticket-based deprovisioning that depends on HR notifying IT of departures","One-time identity proofing at hire without periodic re-verification","Provisioning from templates that pre-assign baseline group membership","Role-change workflows that keep prior-role entitlements during a 30-day transition"],a:0,e:["Correct. When removal depends on a manual handoff from HR, departures get missed and accounts remain active after their owners are gone.","Incorrect. Weak re-verification risks impersonation, but it does not leave accounts behind. It fits concerns about whether the person is who they claim.","Incorrect. Templates speed creation and usually reduce errors, but they have no influence on whether accounts are removed later. Orphaned accounts come from offboarding gaps, not from how accounts are created.","Incorrect. Keeping prior-role entitlements for 30 days produces temporary privilege creep for current employees, not accounts belonging to people who left. It would be the answer to a question about mover risk."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"An application supports only its own local username and password form, with no header or federation support, and the security team wants users not to handle those credentials. What is the BEST interim integration with the SSO portal?",c:["A reverse proxy that passes the authenticated username in an HTTP header the application trusts","A nightly synchronization of AD passwords into the application's local database","Credential vaulting that injects stored passwords into the login form via the SSO portal","LDAP bind from the application to the domain controllers for authentication"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Header-based authentication requires the application to trust the header, which this app cannot do. It fits apps built for proxy authentication.","Incorrect. Synchronizing passwords means users still type them and the hashes are copied to a second store, which weakens rather than centralizes control.","Correct. Injection gives single sign-on behavior without any app changes, and users never see the managed passwords, matching the stem's constraints.","Incorrect. The app does not support directory integration, so this option is not available. It would be ideal if the app offered LDAP or Kerberos."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A company requires managers to approve access requests and a separate team to provision them, so no one person can both approve and implement. Which principle does this apply?",c:["Principle of least privilege","Job rotation across teams","Separation of duties (SoD)","Mandatory vacation policy"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Least privilege limits the rights each person holds. It does not divide the stages of a process among different people.","Incorrect. Rotation moves staff between roles periodically to expose fraud. It does not require different people to handle different stages simultaneously.","Correct. Splitting approval from implementation prevents any single person from completing the whole sensitive process, which is separation of duties.","Incorrect. Forced leave lets others review an employee's work while absent. It detects abuse after the fact rather than splitting duties."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"For which scenario is identity proofing at a HIGH assurance level MOST necessary?",c:["Issuing an online credential for a customer's brokerage account and wires","Registering a visitor on the guest Wi-Fi captive portal using a one-day voucher","Creating a self-registered account on the company's public support forum","Provisioning a read-only lobby kiosk badge for a visiting contractor"],a:0,e:["Correct. Significant financial impact from issuing the credential to the wrong person demands strong, verified proof of real-world identity.","Incorrect. Guest Wi-Fi grants minimal rights and is typically self-asserted or voucher-based, so high-assurance proofing is unnecessary. Higher assurance matters when a credential unlocks sensitive data or money.","Incorrect. Impersonating a forum user has limited consequence, so email verification is typical. It would warrant more assurance only for accounts with moderator powers.","Incorrect. A read-only lobby badge is limited and revocable, and proofing is generally satisfied by the sponsor's attestation. Strong proofing is reserved for credentials with major financial or data impact."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"On a shared file system, each project owner must decide who may collaborate on their folders. Which model fits BEST?",c:["Mandatory access control (MAC)","Role-based access control (RBAC)","Attribute-based access control (ABAC)","Discretionary access control (DAC)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. MAC removes owner discretion in favor of system-enforced labels. It fits environments needing strict classification enforcement.","Incorrect. RBAC ties permissions to roles managed centrally, not to individual owners' choices. It fits standardized job functions.","Incorrect. ABAC evaluates policies built from attributes and is administered centrally rather than by each owner. It fits dynamic, context-aware decisions.","Correct. Owners decide access to the resources they own, which is discretionary access control and matches the requirement exactly."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"After a user changes their password, an attacker's stolen OAuth refresh token continues minting new access tokens. What is the BEST mitigation?",c:["Enforce a longer password policy, block password reuse, and require annual resets","Revoke refresh tokens on password change and risk events, rotate on use, shorten access-token lifetime","Require an SMS one-time code on every API call made with the access token and refresh token","Enable PKCE on the authorization code flow for the public mobile client"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Password changes do not invalidate already-issued tokens, which is exactly the problem shown. Strong passwords protect the login step.","Correct. Revoking and rotating refresh tokens ends the attacker's ability to mint access tokens, and short access-token lifetimes limit the damage window.","Incorrect. Per-call codes are impractical for APIs and SMS is weak. The attacker's token is accepted before any such control applies.","Incorrect. PKCE prevents interception of the authorization code, not reuse of an already-stolen refresh token. It fits mobile and single-page app flows."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Each quarter, application owners are asked to confirm that every user's entitlement is still required, and unconfirmed access is removed. What is this process called?",c:["Role mining and role engineering","Access recertification campaign","Just-in-time access provisioning","Privileged session recording review"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Role mining analyzes existing permissions to design roles. It supports RBAC engineering but does not involve owners confirming access.","Correct. Owners periodically confirm that each user's access remains required and unconfirmed access is removed, which is access recertification (attestation).","Incorrect. Just-in-time provisioning grants access at the time of need and expires it. It reduces standing access but is not a periodic confirmation.","Incorrect. Reviewing recorded admin sessions checks what privileged users did, not whether their access should exist."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A company using RBAC has accumulated hundreds of overlapping roles. What is the main risk and remedy?",c:["Overlapping roles hide leaver accounts, so orphaned accounts accumulate; disable inactive accounts monthly","Overlapping roles share approvers, so separation-of-duties checks fail open; add a second approver","Large role sets slow token issuance, causing authentication latency; cache role lookups","Role explosion makes reviews impractical and erodes least privilege; consolidate through role engineering"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Orphaned accounts come from weak offboarding, not role count. Inactivity sweeps help but do not fix the role model.","Incorrect. Approver structure is separate from role count. SoD conflicts matter, but the main consequence of role sprawl is reviewability.","Incorrect. Token issuance performance is rarely the issue at this scale. The concern with hundreds of roles is governance complexity, not latency.","Correct. Too many overlapping roles hide excessive access and make recertification unworkable, so the remedy is to rationalize and redesign the role model."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A user signs in interactively to a Windows domain with a certificate on a smart card. Which mechanism is used under the hood?",c:["Kerberos with PKINIT extension","EAP-TLS with client certificates","NTLMv2 challenge-response hashes","CHAP challenge-response over PPP"],a:0,e:["Correct. Smart card logon uses public-key initial authentication within Kerberos to obtain the ticket-granting ticket.","Incorrect. EAP-TLS uses certificates for 802.1X network access, not interactive domain logon. It would be right for wired or wireless port authentication.","Incorrect. NTLMv2 relies on password-derived hashes, not certificates. It serves as a fallback when Kerberos is unavailable rather than the mechanism for smart card logon.","Incorrect. CHAP is a password-based challenge protocol used by PPP links. It does not use certificates or smart cards, so it cannot underlie smart card logon."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"In an IAM workflow, which control PREVENTS a user from approving their own access request?",c:["Step-up MFA required on the approval action for every approver","A quarterly access recertification campaign run by application owners","A workflow rule enforcing separation of duties between requester and approver","Session recording of the approver's activity in the IAM portal"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. MFA confirms identity, but the requester is still the approver. It fits protecting sensitive approvals from account takeover.","Incorrect. A recertification campaign is detective and runs after the fact, while the question requires prevention. It fits catching inappropriate access that slipped through earlier.","Correct. A preventive workflow rule that blocks the requester from also being the approver stops self-approval before it can happen.","Incorrect. Session recording provides evidence after the event and does not block self-approval, which the stem asks to prevent. It fits monitoring privileged administrators."]},

// ---------- 4.7 (10) ----------
{o:"4.7",q:"A SOAR playbook automatically disables accounts flagged by a new, untested UEBA rule. It locks out the CEO during a board meeting. What is the MOST important lesson?",c:["Replace automated response with manual analyst review for every alert","Raise the UEBA risk threshold but keep automatic disabling for all accounts","Test automations first and add guardrails or approval for high-impact actions","Exempt executive accounts from all automated playbooks permanently"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Removing automation entirely discards the speed benefits for routine, low-risk steps like enrichment. Full manual review is appropriate only for the highest-impact actions, not every alert in the queue.","Incorrect. A higher threshold reduces how often the rule fires but not the blast radius, and it leaves the lack of testing and oversight untouched. The next false positive would still disable an account with no human check.","Correct. Disruptive actions triggered by an unvalidated detection need testing, thresholds and approval gates, so a false positive cannot lock out the CEO mid-meeting. Guardrails keep the automation useful without unbounded blast radius.","Incorrect. Executive accounts are high-value targets, so a permanent blanket exemption creates a blind spot attackers will seek out. Narrow approval rules for sensitive accounts are better than exempting them from all playbooks."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"A security team connects a dozen tools through scripts and APIs. When one connector fails, alerts stop flowing and nobody can tell where. Which consideration does this BEST illustrate?",c:["Complexity: interdependencies make failures hard to trace and can cascade","Cost: licensing for connectors exceeds the labor savings from automation","Ongoing supportability: the scripts depend on one engineer who understands them","Single point of failure: all alerting depends on one SIEM server"],a:0,e:["Correct. Many interconnected components obscure root causes and let a single connector failure spread, which is the complexity consideration. The stem's untraceable alert loss is a classic symptom of tangled dependencies.","Incorrect. Cost concerns the economics of the program, such as licensing versus labor savings, not diagnosability. It would be the answer if finance were questioning the investment rather than analysts failing to trace a fault.","Incorrect. Supportability concerns who can maintain and update the solution over time. The stem describes difficulty tracing a failure across connected tools, not dependence on particular staff or vendors.","Incorrect. A single point of failure is one component whose loss halts a service. Here the problem is that many interdependent tools make the failing link hard to find, which is a complexity issue."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"The team repeatedly chose quick one-off scripts over a maintainable design to save time, and now every change takes longer and breaks something else. Which concept describes the accumulating cost of those choices?",c:["Single point of failure (SPOF)","Configuration drift across systems","Shadow IT outside team governance","Accumulated technical debt"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A single point of failure is one component whose failure halts a service. The stem describes growing maintenance burden from past design shortcuts, not an outage from one failed component.","Incorrect. Configuration drift is systems diverging from their intended baseline over time. It would fit servers slowly differing from a hardened template, not a team repeatedly choosing quick scripts over maintainable design.","Incorrect. Shadow IT is technology deployed outside IT's governance by other groups. The scripts here were written by the security team itself, so the problem is design shortcuts rather than unsanctioned tools.","Correct. Shortcuts that save effort now create compounding rework and risk later, so each change gets slower and more fragile. That accumulated cost of earlier expedient choices is technical debt."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"A cloud team must PREVENT non-compliant resources, such as public storage buckets, from being created at all. Which approach BEST enforces secure baselines at scale?",c:["Quarterly CSPM scans that report non-compliant resources to their owners for remediation","Policy-as-code guardrails in the pipeline that reject non-compliant resources at creation","Golden-image templates published to the catalog with documentation encouraging their use","Change advisory board review of every cloud deployment request"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Periodic scanning is detective and leaves exposed resources live until someone remediates them. It fits discovering drift in existing environments, but the stem requires that non-compliant resources never be created.","Correct. Enforcing rules in the pipeline blocks bad configurations before they exist, which scales with automation and meets the requirement to prevent creation rather than clean up later.","Incorrect. Published templates are voluntary and do not stop engineers from deploying other configurations. They make the compliant path easy but do not enforce it, which the stem requires.","Incorrect. Manual board review is slow, inconsistent and unenforceable across thousands of cloud deployments. It suits infrequent, high-risk changes rather than baseline enforcement at cloud scale."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"A company depends on a single SOAR platform. When it fails for a day, no alerts are triaged. Which consideration was overlooked?",c:["Return on investment of the SOAR platform license","Single point of failure without a manual fallback","Technical debt in the playbook library and connectors","Ongoing supportability of vendor patch cycles"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Return on investment concerns financial justification, which has no bearing on why triage stopped during the outage. It would apply if leadership were debating whether to renew the SOAR contract.","Correct. A lone platform with no runbooks or redundancy halted triage entirely for a day, which is the single-point-of-failure consideration. Manual fallback procedures and platform resilience were what was missing.","Incorrect. Technical debt makes maintenance harder over time through accumulated shortcuts. The stem describes loss of the platform itself and no fallback, not messy or fragile playbooks.","Incorrect. Supportability covers ongoing upkeep such as patching and staff knowledge. It does not address what the team should do when the service is down, which is a resilience and fallback question."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"Why integrate a ticketing system with SIEM alerts through APIs?",c:["It cuts SIEM licensing costs by shifting log retention to tickets","It lets each ticket trigger automatic firewall blocks of source IPs","It enriches each indicator with threat intelligence reputation data","Each alert gets an owner, SLA clock and audit trail in one record"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A ticketing system is not a log store, and retention is a separate design decision. Offloading retention is not the purpose of the integration and would harm search and compliance.","Incorrect. Automatically blocking source IPs is orchestration performed by SOAR playbooks that call firewall APIs. Ticketing alone records and assigns work and would not take containment actions.","Incorrect. Threat intelligence enrichment is a SOAR or threat intelligence platform function. A ticketing system tracks work and ownership rather than adding context to indicators.","Correct. Tickets give every alert an owner, an SLA clock and an audit trail, providing accountability and metrics for incident handling. That tracking is the main reason to integrate ticketing with the SIEM."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"Automatically creating accounts from HR records reduces errors. Which NEW risk arises from relying on the HR system as the authoritative data source?",c:["Errors or tampering in HR data replicate instantly into accounts, so inputs must be validated and monitored","Provisioning connectors need standing admin rights, so a compromised one can create privileged accounts","Automated accounts cannot enroll in MFA because HR systems do not manage authentication factors","Deprovisioning can no longer be audited because account creation is automated and unlogged"],a:0,e:["Correct. Automation amplifies bad input, so errors or tampering in HR data replicate instantly into accounts unless inputs are validated and monitored. The risk comes from trusting the authoritative source blindly.","Incorrect. This is a real tooling risk, since connectors need privileges, but it concerns the connector rather than the quality of the data source. It would be the answer if the stem asked about securing service accounts.","Incorrect. MFA enrollment is handled at first login or by identity workflows and is independent of HR data. It is not a standard limitation of HR-driven provisioning and does not follow from the data source.","Incorrect. Automated workflows can log both creation and removal, so auditability is typically improved rather than lost. Poor logging would be a configuration issue, not an inherent result of automation."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"Which task is the MOST appropriate candidate for full automation without human approval?",c:["Disabling a vice president's account after a medium-confidence UEBA alert","Quarantining a production database server after an IDS alert","Enriching an alert with WHOIS, reputation and asset-owner data","Blocking an entire /16 range at the perimeter after a scan alert"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A false positive would disrupt a senior user and carry business impact, so human confirmation is warranted. Full automation fits only high-confidence signals with limited consequences.","Incorrect. Isolating a production database can cause an outage if the alert is wrong. Approval is advisable unless confidence is high and the business impact is well understood and accepted.","Correct. Enrichment with WHOIS, reputation and owner data is repetitive, read-only and low-risk, so it is safe to automate end to end without approval. A wrong result costs analyst time, not an outage.","Incorrect. A broad /16 block can cut off legitimate customers or partners from one scan alert. Such wide-reaching changes should be reviewed by a person, or at least limited by safeguards."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"Which statement about the ongoing supportability of security automation is MOST accurate?",c:["Validated playbooks stay valid until the SOAR vendor retires the product version","Commercial SOAR vendors maintain customer-built playbooks as part of standard support","API changes, upgrades and staff turnover can break automations, so owners and tests are needed","Supportability concerns apply to custom code but not to low-code playbooks built in the vendor console"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Integrations break whenever connected tools change their APIs or schemas, regardless of the SOAR vendor's lifecycle. Playbooks therefore need regular re-testing rather than being trusted indefinitely.","Incorrect. Vendors maintain their own product and prebuilt content, not the custom logic your team writes. Responsibility for keeping customer-built playbooks working stays with your own staff.","Correct. Automations depend on external APIs, tools and people, all of which change, so owners and tests are required to keep them working. Planning for maintenance is part of ongoing supportability.","Incorrect. Low-code workflows still rely on APIs, credentials and decision logic that change over time. They need ownership, testing and documentation just like conventional scripts."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"Which outcome shows how automation MOST improves security during employee onboarding?",c:["Baseline access, MFA enrollment and training are assigned consistently on day one","New hires receive temporary administrative access until role-based access is finalized","Managers pick any access on the request form without further approval to speed things up","Background screening is deferred until after accounts are created to avoid delays"],a:0,e:["Correct. Automation applies the same secure baseline to every new user without manual variation, so access, MFA enrollment and training are consistent on day one. That repeatability is its clearest onboarding benefit.","Incorrect. Temporary administrative access violates least privilege and creates risk while access is finalized. Automation should deliver baseline access promptly, not blanket privilege that must later be cleaned up.","Incorrect. Removing approval speeds setup but invites over-provisioning and unreviewed access. Automated workflows should still include approvals for anything beyond the baseline entitlement set.","Incorrect. Screening exists to reduce risk before access is granted, so deferring it until after account creation reverses the intended order. Automation can trigger screening earlier rather than skip it."]},

// ---------- 4.8 (16) ----------
{o:"4.8",q:"An analyst confirms an active intruder on a domain controller. Management wants every server shut down immediately. Why might that be the WRONG first step?",c:["Shutdown is a form of eradication, and eradication must wait until the root cause has been fully documented","Only forensic staff may power systems off without breaking the chain of custody","Domain controllers must first be demoted from the domain before any shutdown is permitted","Powering off destroys volatile evidence and may tip off the attacker; isolate and capture memory first"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Powering off is a crude form of containment, not eradication, so phase ordering is not the real objection. The actual problems are lost volatile evidence and the unnecessary outage of every server.","Incorrect. Chain of custody concerns the documented handling of collected evidence, not who may power a server down. Staff permissions are not why a mass shutdown is a poor first step.","Incorrect. Domain controllers can be powered off without first being demoted, since demotion is a planned decommissioning step. The shutdown is a poor idea because of lost evidence, not a technical prerequisite.","Correct. Powering off destroys volatile memory evidence and can tip off the attacker, so targeted isolation with memory capture balances containment against evidence preservation and needless outage."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"During containment of a worm, the team blocks SMB between VLANs and isolates infected hosts. Which phase comes NEXT?",c:["Recovery: restore systems to production and monitor for recurrence","Eradication: remove the worm and close the exploited flaw","Post-incident activity: document lessons learned and update the plan","Detection and analysis: identify indicators and scope of compromise"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Recovery follows eradication, because restoring systems before the worm and its entry point are gone risks immediate reinfection. It becomes correct once the worm is removed and the flaw is fixed.","Correct. After the spread is stopped by blocking SMB and isolating hosts, the worm and the flaw it used are removed. That is eradication, which precedes recovery.","Incorrect. Lessons learned happens after recovery, once the incident is over. Doing it now would leave the infection in place and the review would lack the final facts.","Incorrect. Detection already occurred, since the team knows the worm exists and has begun containing it. Analysis continues in parallel, but the next formal phase after containment is eradication."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Which activity belongs to RECOVERY rather than eradication?",c:["Deleting malware binaries and persistence mechanisms from hosts","Restoring systems from clean backups and monitoring for recurrence","Removing attacker-created accounts and rotating compromised credentials","Patching the vulnerability the attacker exploited"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Deleting malware binaries and persistence is eradication, which removes the threat from the environment. Recovery starts only after that work is complete.","Correct. Recovery returns systems to production and verifies they stay clean, which means restoring from clean backups and monitoring for recurrence. It comes after the threat has been fully removed.","Incorrect. Removing attacker accounts and rotating compromised credentials strips the attacker's footholds, which is part of eradication. It would not be listed as a recovery task.","Incorrect. Patching the exploited flaw closes the root cause so the attacker cannot return, which is eradication. Recovery restores service and watches for signs of reentry."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"In a lessons-learned review, the team learns detection took 12 days because VPN logs were never sent to the SIEM. What is the BEST outcome?",c:["Reprimand the VPN administrator and add a weekly manual log-review task","Replace the VPN with a different vendor product that includes built-in alerting","Contract a managed SOC provider to monitor all collected logs around the clock","Onboard VPN logs to the SIEM, build detections, and update the IR plan for future preparation"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Blame discourages honest reporting, and a manual weekly review does not scale or build detections. Process failures need process fixes, not punishment for one administrator.","Incorrect. A new VPN product does not address the missing log integration and is costly, while the existing logs could simply be forwarded. Replacement fits cases where the product is truly deficient.","Incorrect. A managed SOC cannot monitor logs that are never collected, so outsourcing leaves the visibility gap in place. It fits capacity gaps rather than coverage gaps.","Correct. Onboarding the logs, building detections and updating the IR plan fixes the visibility gap and closes the loop on preparation, which is the purpose of a lessons-learned review."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"An analyst images a disk and records a SHA-256 hash. Weeks later in court, the defense challenges whether the image is unchanged. What BEST demonstrates integrity?",c:["Re-hashing the image with SHA-256 to match the recorded value, plus chain-of-custody records","Comparing the image's creation timestamp to the acquisition log and analyst notes","Having a second analyst check the original drive's serial number against the evidence tag","Presenting the acquisition tool's vendor certification together with a signed analyst report"],a:0,e:["Correct. Recalculating the hash and matching the recorded value proves the content is unchanged, and chain-of-custody records show who handled it. Together they answer an integrity challenge in court.","Incorrect. Timestamps can be altered and do not prove content integrity. They corroborate the timeline of acquisition but are not evidence that the image is unmodified.","Incorrect. A serial number identifies the physical device and supports provenance, but says nothing about whether the image contents changed. Only a hash comparison shows that.","Incorrect. Tool certification supports the reliability of the acquisition method but does not show the image is unchanged since. It complements hashing rather than replacing it."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Which sequence correctly lists evidence from MOST to LEAST volatile?",c:["RAM, CPU registers and cache, disk, swap and temp files, then archival media, then remote logs","Disk, RAM, swap and temp files, CPU cache, remote logs, archival media","CPU registers and cache, RAM, swap and temp files, disk, remote logs, archival media","Swap and temp files, RAM, registers, remote logs, disk, archival media"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Registers and cache change faster than RAM, so RAM cannot come first, and swap and temp files precede disk. It also places archival media before remote logs, which is wrong.","Incorrect. Disk is far less volatile than RAM and cache, so collecting it first risks losing memory artifacts. This ordering would be appropriate only for a powered-off system.","Correct. Evidence that disappears fastest is collected first, from registers and cache through RAM, swap, disk and remote logs to archival media. This ordering preserves the most volatile data.","Incorrect. This misorders swap, RAM and registers, and places remote logs before disk. Only the final position of archival media is right."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"A company receives a litigation notice while IT continues its routine 30-day email auto-deletion. What is the risk and the fix?",c:["Chain-of-custody violation; forensically image every mailbox before each deletion run","Data sovereignty violation; relocate mailboxes to servers in the plaintiff's jurisdiction","Spoliation of evidence; apply a legal hold suspending deletion for relevant custodians","Attorney-client privilege waiver; encrypt archived mail until counsel completes review"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Chain of custody concerns documented handling of collected evidence. Imaging every mailbox is excessive, and the actual issue is routine deletion of potentially relevant data.","Incorrect. Data sovereignty concerns where data resides under local law, not duties to preserve evidence. Relocating mailboxes would not stop the auto-deletion that creates the legal exposure.","Correct. Destroying data after notice of litigation is spoliation and can bring sanctions, and a legal hold preserves relevant data by suspending routine deletion for the affected custodians.","Incorrect. Privilege concerns the disclosure of protected communications. Encrypting archived mail does nothing to stop automatic deletion of messages that may be relevant."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Which activity is BEST described as threat hunting rather than incident response?",c:["Searching endpoint telemetry for unusual PowerShell use from a hypothesis, with no alert","Isolating a workstation from the network after an EDR alert flags credential dumping","Restoring file servers from clean backups after ransomware encrypted their shares","Interviewing the employee who reported a suspicious phishing email to the help desk"],a:0,e:["Correct. Hunting is proactive and hypothesis-driven, looking for adversary behavior that detections missed, with no alert driving it. Searching for unusual PowerShell is exactly that.","Incorrect. Reacting to an EDR alert by isolating a host is incident response containment. A hunt starts without an alert, from a hypothesis about attacker behavior.","Incorrect. Restoring servers from backup after ransomware is the recovery phase of response, taking place after an incident has been declared and contained.","Incorrect. Interviewing a reporting employee is incident response triage on a known, reported event. Hunting proactively searches for activity nobody has reported."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"A tabletop exercise reveals that nobody knows when to notify regulators after a breach. Which document needs updating?",c:["Business continuity plan (BCP) for critical functions","Disaster recovery plan (DRP) for IT system restoration","Data retention policy (record retention schedules)","Incident response plan (communication section)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A business continuity plan covers keeping critical business functions running during disruption. It would be updated if the tabletop had revealed gaps in operating through an outage.","Incorrect. A disaster recovery plan addresses restoring IT systems after major failures. It does not govern who must notify regulators after a breach.","Incorrect. Retention policies define how long data is kept and when it is destroyed. They do not assign notification responsibilities or timelines.","Correct. Notification roles, timelines and contacts belong in the incident response plan's communication procedures, so updating that plan closes the gap the tabletop exposed."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Malware has been removed from the affected hosts. Why is root cause analysis still important?",c:["It identifies which backups are clean so restoration can begin sooner","Unless the initial access vector is fixed, the attacker can return the same way","It satisfies chain-of-custody requirements so evidence is admissible","It determines the incident severity category used to trigger escalation"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Validating which backups are clean is part of recovery planning and can proceed separately. It is not the main reason root cause analysis remains important after removal.","Correct. Removing malware treats symptoms, while closing the initial access vector prevents the attacker from walking back in. Root cause analysis finds that vector, so it matters even after cleanup.","Incorrect. Chain of custody concerns documented handling of evidence and has no bearing on determining how the attacker got in. Root cause analysis is an analytical step, not a custody step.","Incorrect. Severity is assessed during triage, before containment decisions are made. Root cause is determined later and informs eradication and prevention, not the escalation category."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"What is the MOST appropriate way to preserve evidence from a compromised running cloud VM?",c:["Terminate the instance after exporting only the application and OS logs","Snapshot disks, capture memory if feasible, export logs, then isolate the instance","Reboot the instance from a golden image and compare file hashes with the snapshot","Stop the instance, which preserves memory in the hypervisor, and detach the volumes"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Termination discards memory and any ephemeral storage, so exporting only application logs is insufficient. It is acceptable only when no evidence from the instance is required.","Correct. Snapshotting disks, capturing memory and exporting logs preserves the state first, and isolating the instance afterward contains it without destroying volatile evidence.","Incorrect. Rebooting erases memory and overwrites volatile artifacts, and hash comparison belongs after acquisition. Restarting from a golden image would also remove the compromised state entirely.","Incorrect. Stopping an instance clears RAM, so memory is lost, and detaching volumes preserves only disk data. It is a half-measure compared with capturing memory before any state change."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"In the e-discovery process, which activity comes FIRST?",c:["Processing collected data into a reviewable, searchable format","Reviewing documents for privilege and responsiveness by counsel","Producing responsive documents to opposing counsel in discovery","Identifying and preserving relevant ESI under a legal hold"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Processing follows collection and converts data into a reviewable form. It happens only after data has been identified, preserved and collected.","Incorrect. Review for privilege and responsiveness occurs after collection and processing, because the documents must exist in a reviewable form first.","Incorrect. Production is a late stage that follows review. Producing first would risk disclosing privileged or non-responsive material.","Correct. Relevant data must be located and protected under a legal hold before processing, review or production can happen. Identification and preservation come first in the e-discovery process."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"A simulation shows the SOC cannot reach the network team after hours. What is the BEST fix?",c:["Update the call tree, escalation contacts and on-call rotation in the IR plan, then retest","Give SOC analysts administrative access to network devices after hours","Outsource after-hours network operations to an MSP with a four-hour response SLA","Add a SOAR playbook that isolates network segments automatically when the team is unreachable"],a:0,e:["Correct. The gap is a communication gap, so updating the call tree, escalation contacts and on-call rotation and then retesting it addresses the finding directly.","Incorrect. Giving analysts administrative access to network devices bypasses the network team and violates least privilege. It might be reasonable only for narrow, pre-approved emergency actions.","Incorrect. A four-hour SLA would not help urgent incidents and adds cost, and it addresses staffing rather than the broken escalation path the exercise exposed.","Incorrect. Automated isolation with no human decision can cause outages and does not fix who gets called. It could supplement a response plan but not replace a working escalation path."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Which statement BEST describes the goal of containment?",c:["Remove malicious artifacts and close the exploited vulnerability","Return affected systems to production and monitor for recurrence","Limit the scope and impact of the incident while preserving evidence","Document the timeline, root cause and improvements for the next iteration"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Removing malicious artifacts and closing the exploited vulnerability describes eradication, which follows containment. It would be the answer if the question asked what happens after the spread is stopped.","Incorrect. Returning systems to production and monitoring for recurrence describes recovery, which follows eradication. It would be the answer if the question asked about restoring operations.","Correct. Containment stops the spread and limits damage without destroying forensic data, so evidence remains available for later analysis. That balance is its defining goal.","Incorrect. Documenting the timeline, root cause and improvements describes post-incident activity or lessons learned, the final phase. It would be the answer if the question asked about improving the plan afterward."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"An analyst attaches a hardware write blocker before imaging a suspect's drive. Why?",c:["To compute a hash of the image in parallel while copying","To encrypt the forensic image so only authorized examiners can open it","To block any writes to the original drive so evidence is not altered","To compress the image and reduce transfer time to the evidence server"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Hashing is performed by the imaging software, not by the blocker. It verifies integrity afterward, whereas the blocker prevents changes in the first place.","Incorrect. Encryption is a storage protection applied to the image and is not the function of a write blocker. A blocker only prevents writes to the source.","Correct. A write blocker prevents any writes to the original drive during imaging, preserving the integrity of the evidence so the copy can be trusted in court.","Incorrect. Compression is an option of imaging tools to save space and time, not a function of write blockers, which exist to protect the source."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Which approach BEST builds the speed and coordination IR staff need during a real ransomware event?",c:["Recurring hands-on simulations with realistic injects and tabletops","An annual compliance slide deck with a short quiz on IR phases","A vendor certification course taken once at hire by each analyst","A detailed written playbook reviewed at the quarterly team meeting"],a:0,e:["Correct. Practicing under realistic pressure builds muscle memory and exposes plan gaps before a real event. Simulations with realistic injects train speed and coordination, which the stem asks for.","Incorrect. A slide deck and quiz test recall but not execution under stress, so it satisfies compliance requirements without building the speed and coordination needed in a real event.","Incorrect. A one-time certification builds knowledge but does not exercise team coordination, and skills fade without practice. It does not prepare a team for a real ransomware response.","Incorrect. Reviewing a document is passive and does not test whether people can carry it out. A playbook supports preparation but does not replace hands-on exercises."]},

// ---------- 4.9 (14) ----------
{o:"4.9",q:"An analyst suspects data exfiltration to a cloud storage provider over HTTPS. Which data sources BEST show WHO sent how much and WHEN, without decrypting content?",c:["DNS query logs plus the DHCP lease table for the host","Firewall deny logs with threat-prevention signature hits","EDR file-creation events plus Windows logon events","Proxy logs with user identity plus NetFlow byte counts"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. DNS and DHCP logs show which host looked up the provider but not how much data was sent. They help attribute an IP address to a device, not measure a transfer.","Incorrect. Deny logs record blocked traffic, so an allowed upload does not appear in them. They fit investigating blocked attacks rather than successful exfiltration.","Incorrect. EDR file events and logons show activity and sessions on the host but not the volume sent to the destination. They fit analyzing data staging, not transfer size.","Correct. The proxy ties authenticated users to destinations and times, and flow data supplies volumes, so together they show who sent how much and when without decrypting the HTTPS content."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"To determine whether malware executed on a Windows host and which parent process launched it, which data source is BEST?",c:["Windows Security Event 4624 logon events with logon type","Sysmon Event 1 or Security Event 4688 process-creation logs","Sysmon Event ID 3 network connection records per process","Firewall session logs with application identification"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Logon events show who authenticated and how, not which processes ran. They fit establishing user session timing but not execution lineage.","Correct. Process creation events record the new process, its parent and command line, so lineage is visible. That is what the question needs to see which process launched the malware.","Incorrect. Network connection events show connections made by a process but not which parent launched it. They fit tracing C2 communication after the process is known.","Incorrect. Firewall session logs show traffic and applications, not host process lineage. They fit network-level scoping of an incident rather than endpoint execution."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An investigator must show that an email actually came from a claimed sender's infrastructure. Which data is MOST useful?",c:["The message body text, attachment hashes and embedded URLs","Full headers: Received chain, SPF/DKIM/DMARC results, Message-ID","The display name, From address and Reply-To shown in the mail client","The recipient server's TLS cipher-suite negotiation log"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Body content and attachment hashes are easy to forge and say nothing about the transmission path. Hashes help identify malware rather than prove where a message came from.","Correct. Full headers show the relay path and the SPF, DKIM and DMARC verdicts, which support or refute the claimed origin and are hard for the sender to forge end to end.","Incorrect. Display name, From and Reply-To are attacker-controlled presentation fields, the very things spoofing manipulates. They cannot establish where the message was sent from.","Incorrect. TLS cipher logs show transport protection between servers, not who originated the message. Encrypted delivery does not prove the sender's identity."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An analyst needs the Windows Security log entry that records a SUCCESSFUL account logon. Which event ID?",c:["Event ID 4625","Event ID 4648","Event ID 4672","Event ID 4624"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. 4625 records a failed logon attempt and is used to spot brute force or password spraying. It would be the answer if the question asked about failures.","Incorrect. 4648 records a logon attempted with explicit credentials, such as runas. It does not by itself denote a normal successful logon event.","Incorrect. 4672 records special privileges being assigned to a new logon, which accompanies admin logons. It signals elevated rights, not the logon event itself.","Correct. Event ID 4624 records a successful logon in the Windows Security log, including the logon type and account, which is what the analyst needs."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"Which source BEST reveals the exact, deobfuscated PowerShell commands an attacker ran, even when passed as Base64-encoded arguments?",c:["PowerShell Script Block Logging (Event ID 4104)","Security Event 4688 process creation logs","Sysmon Event ID 3 network connection logs","PSReadLine history file (ConsoleHost_history.txt) on disk"],a:0,e:["Correct. Script block logging records the code actually executed after PowerShell decodes it, so Base64-encoded commands become readable. It reveals what the attacker really ran.","Incorrect. Process creation logs capture the command line, which in this case would show only the encoded blob. They identify that PowerShell ran but not what the script did.","Incorrect. Network connection events show where processes connected, not which commands were run. They fit identifying C2 destinations rather than reconstructing commands.","Incorrect. The PSReadLine history file records only interactive typed commands, not scripts or remote sessions, and attackers can delete it. It is unreliable for reconstructing everything that ran."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An analyst sees one host issuing hundreds of DNS queries that return NXDOMAIN for random-looking domains. What does this MOST likely suggest?",c:["DNS tunneling exfiltrating data in long subdomain labels","Fast-flux hosting rotating A records for a single phishing domain","Malware using a domain generation algorithm to find its C2","Typosquatting by a user mistyping popular brand names"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. DNS tunneling encodes data in subdomains of one attacker-controlled domain, producing many queries to a single zone. The NXDOMAIN spread across many random domains points to a DGA instead.","Incorrect. Fast flux rotates IP answers for a domain that resolves successfully, so it produces answers, not NXDOMAIN responses for random names.","Correct. A domain generation algorithm tries many generated names until one resolves, producing many failed lookups from one host. The NXDOMAIN pattern for random-looking names points to a DGA seeking its C2.","Incorrect. A person mistyping a name produces a few failed lookups, not hundreds of random-looking strings from a single host in a short time."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"Which log definitively shows the URL requested, source IP and response code for requests to a web application's admin page?",c:["DHCP server lease logs for the client subnet","Database audit logs for the application schema","Web server or application access logs for the site","WAF blocked-request logs for the application"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. DHCP logs map IP addresses to devices but do not record requested URLs. They help identify the internal host behind an address, not what it requested.","Incorrect. Database audit logs record queries on the data store, not HTTP requests. They would help assess what an admin session touched after the page was accessed.","Correct. Web server or application access logs record each request with the path, client IP and status code, which is what an admin-page investigation needs.","Incorrect. WAF blocked-request logs list only requests matched by rules, so successful requests may not appear. They fit analyzing attack attempts rather than confirming who reached the page."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"Which Word document metadata fields could help identify the insider who created a leaked file?",c:["Author and Last Modified By properties","Created-date and Total editing time statistics","Template path and installed add-in list","Last printed date and word count"],a:0,e:["Correct. Office metadata often records the usernames of those who created and last edited the file, which can point to the insider who produced the leaked document.","Incorrect. Created date and editing time give timing, which helps place the work but does not name a person. They corroborate an account rather than identify a user.","Incorrect. Template paths and add-ins describe the software environment, which only indirectly points to a user or machine. They are weak identifiers compared with author fields.","Incorrect. Last printed date and word count describe the content and usage, not who created it. They have little value for attribution."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"Which statement about full packet capture in an investigation is MOST accurate?",c:["It is inexpensive to retain for months because only packet headers are stored","It can decrypt past TLS sessions later using the server's current certificate key","It replaces endpoint logs because it records user identity and process names","It gives full payload detail but is storage-heavy and limited when traffic is encrypted"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Header-only capture describes flow records, which are cheap to keep. Full capture stores payloads and consumes large amounts of storage.","Incorrect. With forward secrecy, a certificate private key cannot decrypt recorded sessions; per-session keys would be needed. Decrypting past sessions with the current certificate does not work.","Incorrect. Packets do not show process or user context, so capture cannot replace endpoint and identity logs. They provide different evidence that must be correlated.","Correct. Full packet capture is rich in detail but expensive to store, and it cannot reveal TLS content without key material. Those trade-offs are why it is used selectively."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An IPS log shows 'ET EXPLOIT Apache Log4j RCE Attempt' blocked from an external IP. Which follow-up is MOST important?",c:["Add the source IP to a permanent block list and close the alert as resolved","Find vulnerable Log4j instances and hunt for outbound LDAP/DNS callbacks that show success","Rotate all service-account passwords across the environment as a precaution","Re-baseline the IPS signature set after the vendor's next quarterly update cycle"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Attackers rotate IP addresses and other attempts may not match the signature, so a block list alone is a minor supplement. Closing the alert without verification risks missing a successful exploit.","Correct. A blocked attempt on one path does not prove others failed, so verify exposure and hunt for signs of exploitation such as outbound callbacks. That confirms whether any attempt succeeded.","Incorrect. A blanket password rotation without evidence of compromise is disruptive and does not address the vulnerability itself. It fits cases where credential theft is confirmed.","Incorrect. Waiting for a scheduled signature update ignores the current exposure. Signature management is routine maintenance, not the urgent follow-up to a Log4j attempt."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"Which data source is MOST useful for reconstructing which individual files a user accessed on a file server?",c:["Network share access events (Event ID 5140) listing share names","Object-access audit events on the file server (Event ID 4663)","Logon events (Event ID 4624) with source workstation names","NetFlow records with SMB byte counts between hosts"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Share access events identify the share touched, not individual files, so they cannot answer the file-level question. They fit establishing that a share was used at all.","Correct. With auditing enabled, object-access events record file-level access by account and time, which is the data needed to reconstruct which files a user opened.","Incorrect. Logon events show who authenticated from which workstation, not what files were opened afterward. They help build the session timeline only.","Incorrect. Flow data shows volume between hosts but not filenames. It fits detecting large transfers, not reconstructing individual file access."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"A SIEM dashboard shows a spike in blocked events from the WAF for one application. What is the BEST next step?",c:["Suppress the blocked-event alert class to reduce noise on the SIEM dashboard","Switch the WAF to detection-only mode to see what the attackers try next","Block the top source IPs at the perimeter and treat the spike as resolved","Drill into WAF logs for attack types, sources and any rule bypasses"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Suppressing the alert class hides the signal and any bypasses within it. Tuning is justified only for sources confirmed benign.","Incorrect. Detection-only mode lets attacks reach the application rather than blocking them. It fits temporary tuning in staging, not a production spike under active attack.","Incorrect. IP blocks may be evaded by rotating addresses and do not show whether anything bypassed the WAF. They are a supplementary action, not an investigation.","Correct. The aggregate spike is a signal to investigate the underlying detail, including attack types, sources and any requests that bypassed rules. Blocked events imply some may not have been blocked."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"Which vulnerability scan data would MOST help an investigator explain how an attacker initially gained entry?",c:["Historical scans showing an unpatched internet-facing service at compromise time","The latest post-remediation scan showing a clean result for the server","An authenticated CIS benchmark compliance scan of the workstation fleet","An asset inventory export listing hardware owners and warranty dates"],a:0,e:["Correct. Historical scans give point-in-time evidence of the exposure that existed when the intrusion happened, such as an unpatched internet-facing service, which explains the initial entry.","Incorrect. A clean post-remediation scan hides what was exposed earlier, so it cannot explain how the attacker got in. It fits verifying that remediation worked.","Incorrect. Workstation compliance results are unrelated to an internet-facing entry point. They fit configuration baselining rather than reconstructing the initial compromise.","Incorrect. An asset inventory identifies assets and owners but not their vulnerabilities at the time of compromise, so it does not show how access was gained."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"Why collect logs from multiple sources (endpoint, network, identity, cloud) during an investigation?",c:["Centralizing multiple logs lets one high-fidelity source replace the others and cut storage","Multiple sources are needed only for compliance reporting, not for investigations","Correlation fills gaps, since attackers may evade one source but not all","Duplicate collection is required so each log hash matches for chain of custody"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. No single source covers every layer, and reducing storage is not the goal. Relying on one high-fidelity source would create blind spots.","Incorrect. Investigations rely on correlation across sources to reconstruct events and test hypotheses. Compliance reporting is a secondary benefit.","Correct. Combining endpoint, network, identity and cloud logs fills gaps and confirms activity that one source missed, since attackers may evade one source but not all of them.","Incorrect. Chain of custody relies on documented handling and hashes of preserved evidence, not on duplicate collection from multiple sources."]}
]);
